\name{define.genome}
\alias{define.genome}
\title{Define a genome from a linkage map and optional chromosome lengths}

\description{
Defines a diploid simulation genome and linkage map from a data frame
of locus positions. The number of chromosomes is inferred from the map.
Chromosome lengths can be supplied explicitly or inferred from the
locus positions.
}

\usage{
define.genome(map, length.vec=NULL)
}

\arguments{
   \item{map}{Linkage map or map object.}
   \item{length.vec}{Vector of chromosome lengths. If a chromosome length is zero, it is replaced by 0.01.}
}

\details{
Dataframe provided to the map function must have three columns and an optional fourth column. 
First four columns are interpreted as:
\emph{Chromosome, Position, Name, Class.}
Column names do not need to be specified.

\describe{
 \item{Chromosome}{
 Numeric chromosome identifier. Values must be whole numbers.
 }

 \item{Position}{
 Numeric position of the locus in Morgans, greater than or equal
 to zero. When chromosome lengths are supplied explicitly, locus
 positions must lie within the corresponding chromosomes.
 }

 \item{Name}{
 Character string identifying the locus. Locus names must be unique.
 }

 \item{Class}{
 Optional character class label. Classes can be used to define
 effects for groups of loci. If this column is omitted, class names
 are generated by prefixing each locus name with \code{"Class"}.
 For example, locus \code{"form"} receives class \code{"Classform"}.
 }
}

Calling this function replaces the simulation genome and linkage map.
Existing simulation populations and effect definitions are removed.
}

\value{
\code{NULL} on successful completion. The function is called for setting genome parameters and defining the linkage map in the backend.
}

\examples{
map <- data.frame(chrom = 1:2,
   pos = c(0.01,0.02),
   name = c("form","color"))
define.genome(map)

map <- data.frame(chrom = 1:2,
   pos = c(0.01,0.02),
   name = c("form","color"),
   class = paste0("trait",1:2))
define.genome(map)

map <- data.frame(chrom = 1:2,
   pos = c(0.01,0.02),
   name = c("form","color"),
   class = paste0("trait",1:2))
define.genome(map, length.vec=c(0.1,0.1))

}
\seealso{
This function is a wrapper for the more explicit [set.genome.par] and [define.map] 
that behave slightly differently.
}